{"connection_id": "BC-7f04e1debf41a96f3de8", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "house of God and gate of heaven", "note": "Jacob calls Bethel the house of God and the gate of heaven; it gives the commentary's final home a threshold where earthly travel meets divine presence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ee7ea7ea38e64d69beb", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.20", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "guarded way", "note": "Jacob's vow asks God to be with him and guard him on the way he travels, a direct individual counterpart to a request for guidance with protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-126a1df85726aeec15b0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.21", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "bayt and bayit: journey to a father's house", "note": "The commentary glosses hidaya as showing the way and the house at its end; Jacob asks to return in peace to his father's bayit, the Hebrew cognate of Arabic bayt, after his journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b0786228fc17edc602f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.21", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "return in peace to father's house", "note": "Jacob seeks to return safely to his father's house; the goal of the guarded road is explicitly a home and restored belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f5cd98ac9d2968d9cee", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.22", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "bayt and bayit: a sacred house", "note": "The commentary's route ends at the House; Jacob names the place where he slept Bet-El, God's house, using the Hebrew cognate of Arabic bayt for a sanctuary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aba6c4e931a45ee237a6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.22", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "house and tithe", "note": "The Bethel stone is to become God's house, and Jacob vows a tithe from what God gives him; sacred dwelling and conveyed gift are conjoined."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e85efb3e11b5332233d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.29.23", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bride delivered to husband", "note": "Laban brings Leah to Jacob at night; the same transfer scene appears here within a deceptive marriage narrative, which keeps the relation from being idealized."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6563a9422aeca5b52c6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.32.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "present prepared for estranged brother", "note": "Jacob selects a gift for Esau before their feared meeting, an actual gift sent ahead to repair a dangerous relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bb47396dcb052e744bb", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gift reconciles estranged brothers", "note": "The commentary says a gift sent to a rival may fail to reach its recipient; Jacob presses Esau to accept a gift after their estrangement, presenting it as a sign of God's favor and reconciliation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ac01ef5990818058662", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gift named a blessing", "note": "Jacob asks Esau to accept his blessing, meaning the present sent ahead; a gift between estranged kin is thus expressed through blessing language."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1b27a2e08286b0df07f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.35.1", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "go to Bethel, dwell, and build altar", "note": "God tells Jacob to arise, go to Bethel, dwell there, and make an altar; route, settlement, and sacrificial worship are inseparable instructions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3029714641611e454d71", "ref": "WLC:Gen.35.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God present on the way", "note": "Jacob says God answered him in distress and was with him on the way, then calls his household to go build an altar at Bethel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b259959d6077a2c3881c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.35.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "altar at House of God", "note": "Jacob builds an altar at Bethel and names the place El-Bethel, making arrival at God's house culminate in sacrificial action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-878a92442542852d27ae", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "protective sign on a vulnerable person", "note": "God puts a sign on Cain so that no one who finds him will kill him; it is a marked person's inviolability, though not an asylum treaty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1a96a2ed2149912b3ebb", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "firstborn flock gift accepted", "note": "Abel brings firstborn animals from his flock and their fat portions, and YHWH regards him and his offering; animal gift and acceptance are explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-562ed915365dfa2b5a1b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "offering not regarded", "note": "Cain's offering is not regarded, providing another scriptural instance in which the relation between giver and recipient, rather than conveyance alone, determines an offering's reception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94d1cd1770dcb2e78499", "ref": "WLC:Gen.43.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "gifts carried to a powerful unknown recipient", "note": "Jacob sends his sons with choice products as a present to the Egyptian ruler, a carried gift aimed at a recipient whose disposition is uncertain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-665a9a71820fdf18012f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fugitives ask for shelter", "note": "The commentary describes a person arriving among a group and seeking protection; Isaiah calls for Moab to hide outcasts and not betray fugitives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32a8e84cbd1c3da0c419", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "shelter for outcasts", "note": "The appeal asks Judah to provide shade like night and hide the outcasts, a vivid shelter image for endangered arrivals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e85084d477492f822258", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the refugee lives in a hiding place", "note": "The commentary's petitioner is protected by the community he approaches; Isaiah asks Moab to let Judah's outcasts dwell there as shelter from the destroyer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ddf7c66d359ac6708c1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moabite outcasts may sojourn", "note": "The speaker asks that Moab's outcasts live with Judah and that Judah be a shelter from the destroyer, closely matching asylum in another community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37e074e2a8ccce5d9b9e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.18.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tribute carried to Name-place", "note": "A gift or tribute is brought to the place of the name of YHWH on Zion, giving a biblical destination for a carried offering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79ec383b80241c794a9f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nations walk to God's mountain", "note": "The commentary pictures guidance whose path ends at a sacred home; Isaiah has peoples go up to YHWH's mountain to learn his ways and walk in his paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b1cdac29b7261a7fa81", "ref": "WLC:Isa.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "House, ways, and paths", "note": "Nations go up to the house of Jacob's God so that he may teach them his ways and they may walk his paths; house and guided way are one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e46de1274cd9bb28d74", "ref": "WLC:Isa.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic Bayt and Hebrew bêt", "note": "The commentary's explicit Beyt or house image corresponds formally to Hebrew bêt in 'house of the God of Jacob'; the verse's actual context supplies ascent and taught paths, not a lexical claim alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e074503672819c7cab2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the Lord's road is prepared", "note": "The commentary's worshippers ask God to guide them along a straight path; Isaiah instead commands a wilderness voice to prepare a straight highway for YHWH's own coming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cee4a015cd5b3e8bc7dc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.56.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "foreigner, house, accepted sacrifice", "note": "God brings foreign adherents to his holy mountain; their offerings are accepted on his altar in a house of prayer, bringing outsider reception and sacrifice together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-637294b1cc17de43d252", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "homeless poor brought into one's house", "note": "Isaiah defines righteous care in part as bringing the homeless poor into one's house, an exact home-making act for a vulnerable outsider."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c5c03a746ba8af73d46", "ref": "WLC:Isa.60.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "camel caravan with gifts", "note": "Camels carry gold and frankincense to Zion while proclaiming YHWH's praise, a caravan image that joins the commentary's camels, carried gift, and exalted name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3162034e193000a2102", "ref": "WLC:Isa.60.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "flocks on altar and glorified house", "note": "Flocks are accepted on God's altar and he glorifies his house; this makes herd animals reach the sanctuary for worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db978a0462aad726eb26", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straight path for returning people", "note": "God leads the returning people by streams on a straight path where they will not stumble; it gives a prophetic form of safe guidance, though the verse names no house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df8dd21729630f0a7b86", "ref": "WLC:Jer.42.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "displaced people ask for a way", "note": "The commentary reads ihdinā as asking for a route that reaches safety; Judah's remnant asks Jeremiah to seek God's direction about the way they should go after Jerusalem's fall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80c48a30a3c4d152daa9", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.32", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "doors opened to the traveler", "note": "Job says no stranger had to lodge in the street because he opened his doors to the traveler, a domestic counterpart to sheltering the one who arrives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-62d85d771217dd0a8c5f", "ref": "WLC:Josh.2.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oath requested for household protection", "note": "The commentary's refugee asks a group for protection or a pledge; Rahab asks the Israelite spies to swear that they will spare her family because she has shown them loyalty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1fffe117425215eb62d", "ref": "WLC:Josh.2.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oath given to an outsider", "note": "The commentary links refuge with a protective covenant; the spies swear to preserve Rahab's life if she keeps their mission secret."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d998e587df4bdd08c4ff", "ref": "WLC:Josh.20.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "admission and a place to dwell", "note": "A fugitive reaches the city gate, states his case to elders, is admitted, and receives a place to live among them; this is especially close to the commentary's seeker of protection."}

{"connection_id":"BC-4dee7d0f24a152ff8244","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.35","status":"rejected","reason":"Abraham's wealth includes herds and camels, but verse does not connect blessing to hedy or bride's journey.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.35"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-ed5ec51c19b589821085","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.53","status":"accepted","reason":"Servant gives Rebekah and family gifts as part of the bridal journey narrated in chapter 24.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.53","WLC:Gen.24.61","WLC:Gen.24.67"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-1d9b1e1457efb7bbd91e","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.61","status":"accepted","reason":"Rebekah and attendants explicitly mount camels and follow servant toward Isaac.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.53","WLC:Gen.24.61","WLC:Gen.24.67"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7a27b6c3f149e712730b","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.67","status":"accepted","reason":"Isaac brings Rebekah into Sarah's tent and takes her as wife there, unlike pre-transfer marriage order.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.53","WLC:Gen.24.61","WLC:Gen.24.67"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-28b1ca808e892fb81415","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.67","status":"accepted","reason":"Bridal journey ends at Isaac's tent, then marriage; annotation preserves that sequence.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.53","WLC:Gen.24.61","WLC:Gen.24.67"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7f04e1debf41a96f3de8","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Bethel called God's house, but no guidance or arrival occurs in this verse; Gen 24:27 names guided route to house.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8ee7ea7ea38e64d69beb","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.20","status":"rejected","reason":"Jacob asks safety on road, but father's house appears only in verse 21; Gen 24:27 joins both directly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.20"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-126a1df85726aeec15b0","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.21","status":"rejected","reason":"Bayit means house, but Arabic bayt is a commentary image, not wording of Qur'an 1:6; lexical comparison adds little.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.21"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8b0786228fc17edc602f","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.21","status":"rejected","reason":"Jacob hopes to return to father's house; Gen 24:27 explicitly says YHWH guided along way to a house.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.21"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5f5cd98ac9d2968d9cee","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.22","status":"rejected","reason":"Bethel stone is vowed as God's house; no guided route, and bayt is not a Qur'anic word in 1:6.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.22"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-aba6c4e931a45ee237a6","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.22","status":"rejected","reason":"House and tithe are vowed, but no gift is physically conveyed there in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.22"],"annotations":[]}

arguments:
text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s001/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec13/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-aba6c4e931a45ee237a6","ref":"WLC:Gen.28.22","status":"rejected","reason":"House and tithe are vowed, but no gift is physically conveyed there in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.28.22"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6e85efb3e11b5332233d","ref":"WLC:Gen.29.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Leah is brought to Jacob at night in a deception, but the verse offers no guided journey to a new home; Gen 24:53,61,67 supplies that sequence.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.29.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a6563a9422aeca5b52c6","ref":"WLC:Gen.32.14","status":"accepted","reason":"Jacob prepares a present for estranged Esau before their dangerous meeting.","paragraphs":[65],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.32.14","WLC:Gen.33.11","WLC:1Sam.25.35"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6bb47396dcb052e744bb","ref":"WLC:Gen.33.11","status":"accepted","reason":"Esau accepts Jacob's pressed gift after estrangement, showing that such gifts can reconcile.","paragraphs":[65],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.32.14","WLC:Gen.33.11","WLC:1Sam.25.35"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4ac01ef5990818058662","ref":"WLC:Gen.33.11","status":"accepted","reason":"Jacob calls his present a blessing and Esau accepts it; the Hebrew gift is not the word for a divine sacrifice.","paragraphs":[65],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.32.14","WLC:Gen.33.11","WLC:1Sam.25.35"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e1b27a2e08286b0df07f","ref":"WLC:Gen.35.1","status":"rejected","reason":"Jacob must go to Bethel and build an altar, but this instruction alone does not give the guided route; Gen 24:27 and Exod 15:13,17 give clearer guidance-to-home scenes.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.35.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3029714641611e454d71","ref":"WLC:Gen.35.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Jacob recalls God's presence on his way and plans a Bethel altar, but guidance and arrival are in separate narrated moments; Exod 15 is closer.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.35.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b259959d6077a2c3881c","ref":"WLC:Gen.35.7","status":"rejected","reason":"An altar is built at Bethel, but the verse does not narrate an animal brought there or a directed route.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.35.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-878a92442542852d27ae","ref":"WLC:Gen.4.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Cain receives a protective sign, but this is a divine mark on a punished individual, not a human covenant granting asylum.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.4.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1a96a2ed2149912b3ebb","ref":"WLC:Gen.4.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Abel's firstborn flock offering is accepted, but there is no journey to sanctuary or rejected rival gift in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.4.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-562ed915365dfa2b5a1b","ref":"WLC:Gen.4.5","status":"rejected","reason":"Cain's offering is not regarded, but it is not a political gift to a rival and the verse gives no transport or house.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.4.5"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-94d1cd1770dcb2e78499","ref":"WLC:Gen.43.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Produce is carried as a present to an Egyptian ruler, but he is unknown rather than an identified adversary; the Sheba narrative is a direct Solomon comparison.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.43.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-665a9a71820fdf18012f","ref":"WLC:Isa.16.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The addressee is asked to hide outcasts and fugitives, but Hebrew text has reading variants and gives no actual covenant or stable residence; Deut 23:16-17 is explicit.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.16.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-32a8e84cbd1c3da0c419","ref":"WLC:Isa.16.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Shade and concealment are appeals for protection, but no grant of refuge is narrated and the Hebrew outcast reading varies; Josh 20:4 states admission.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.16.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e85084d477492f822258","ref":"WLC:Isa.16.4","status":"rejected","reason":"The outcasts are urged to sojourn under shelter, but who owns them is ambiguous in Hebrew; Deut 23:17 securely names chosen residence.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.16.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9ddf7c66d359ac6708c1","ref":"WLC:Isa.16.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Competing identifications of the outcasts make a Moab-to-Judah asylum claim insecure; Josh 20:4 and Deut 23:17 are clearer.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.16.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-37e074e2a8ccce5d9b9e","ref":"WLC:Isa.18.7","status":"rejected","reason":"A tribute reaches Zion, place of YHWH's name, but no herd animal or cultic sacrifice is specified; Deut 12:11 is closer to the hedy image.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.18.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-79ec383b80241c794a9f","ref":"WLC:Isa.2.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Nations first ascend to God's house, then learn his ways; the commentary's guided path ending at home has the reverse movement. Exod 15:13,17 is closer.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.2.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8b1cdac29b7261a7fa81","ref":"WLC:Isa.2.3","status":"rejected","reason":"House and paths occur together, but the path learned after ascent is moral conduct, not a guided journey to the House as in Exod 15.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.2.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4e46de1274cd9bb28d74","ref":"WLC:Isa.2.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Hebrew bet and Arabic bayt are cognate, but bayt occurs in the commentary's explanation, not Qur'an 1:6, so this is not a direct Qur'anic lexical link.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.2.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4e074503672819c7cab2","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The commanded straight wilderness road is for YHWH's coming, whereas the commentary asks God to guide worshippers to a home; direction and actor differ.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cee4a015cd5b3e8bc7dc","ref":"WLC:Isa.56.7","status":"accepted","reason":"God brings foreign adherents to holy mountain and accepts their sacrifices in his house of prayer; verse 6 identifies them and covenant service.","paragraphs":[65],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.56.6","WLC:Isa.56.7"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-010"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-637294b1cc17de43d252","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Inviting the homeless poor into one's house is concrete hospitality, but no covenant, animal offering or divine destination appears; Deut 23:17 and Isa 56:6-7 address those links.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6c5c03a746ba8af73d46","ref":"WLC:Isa.60.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Camel caravan brings gold and frankincense to Zion, but these are not hedy animals; 1 Kgs 10:2,10,13 gives the pointed Solomon counter-narrative.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.60.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b3162034e193000a2102","ref":"WLC:Isa.60.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Flocks are accepted at God's altar and his house is glorified, but no route or named season is given; Deut 12:11 combines offered animals and chosen place.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.60.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-db978a0462aad726eb26","ref":"WLC:Jer.31.9","status":"rejected","reason":"God leads returnees on a straight path without stumbling, but the verse does not name a house; Ps 107:7 reaches an inhabited city and Exod 15:17 the sanctuary.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Jer.31.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-df8dd21729630f0a7b86","ref":"WLC:Jer.42.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Displaced survivors ask which way to go, but they have no specified home or destination in this verse; Gen 24:27 joins guidance to a house.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Jer.42.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-80c48a30a3c4d152daa9","ref":"WLC:Job.31.32","status":"rejected","reason":"Job opens doors to travelers, but hospitality is personal charity, without covenantal or sacred-house movement.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Job.31.32"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-62d85d771217dd0a8c5f","ref":"WLC:Josh.2.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Rahab asks spies for an oath to spare her household, but she already inhabits the house; the commentary pictures a newcomer asking admission.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.2.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d1fffe117425215eb62d","ref":"WLC:Josh.2.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Spies promise Rahab protection conditional on secrecy, but this is a rescue bargain for an existing home, not a refuge-seeker given dwelling.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.2.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d998e587df4bdd08c4ff","ref":"WLC:Josh.20.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Fugitive reaches city gate, states case, is admitted by elders and receives a place to dwell, closely matching the petition scene.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.23.16","WLC:Deut.23.17","WLC:Josh.20.4","WLC:Josh.9.15","WLC:Josh.9.19","WLC:Exod.21.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f03446a35746b7e5121c","ref":"WLC:Josh.24.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Joshua speaks of serving YHWH with his household, but no house as destination, sacrifice or road occurs in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7a5c71c1553766f35dbc","ref":"WLC:Josh.6.25","status":"rejected","reason":"Rahab remains within Israel's community, but the text says she and family were spared, not that a stranger arrived for pledged asylum; Josh 20:4 is closer.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.6.25"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4434b59c2ce64a3b9b3c","ref":"WLC:Josh.9.15","status":"accepted","reason":"Joshua makes peace and covenant with Gibeonites to let them live, a direct protective agreement with outsiders.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.23.16","WLC:Deut.23.17","WLC:Josh.20.4","WLC:Josh.9.15","WLC:Josh.9.19","WLC:Exod.21.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ff808a83f679a5ee48a0","ref":"WLC:Josh.9.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The leaders' sworn covenant grants the Gibeonites life, although their ruse makes this a qualified analogy.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.23.16","WLC:Deut.23.17","WLC:Josh.20.4","WLC:Josh.9.15","WLC:Josh.9.19","WLC:Exod.21.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-33a7f67cd5a5824bbd66","ref":"WLC:Josh.9.18","status":"rejected","reason":"Israel does not strike Gibeonites because leaders swore, but verse 19 states obligation explicitly and is cited in the annotation.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.9.18"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

output.text:
{"connection_id": "BC-f03446a35746b7e5121c", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "household service in עבד", "note": "Joshua's 'my house will serve YHWH' uses Hebrew עבד, cognate with the commentary's عبادت family, and locates worshipful service within a household declaration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a5c71c1553766f35dbc", "ref": "WLC:Josh.6.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Rahab received into Israel", "note": "Rahab and her family are spared and she dwells in Israel because of the oath-bound protection she gave the spies; asylum, covenant, and communal incorporation coincide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4434b59c2ce64a3b9b3c", "ref": "WLC:Josh.9.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "covenant preserves the outsiders", "note": "The commentary links an asylum-seeker with a pledge from the group; Joshua makes peace and a covenant with Gibeon, letting its people live."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff808a83f679a5ee48a0", "ref": "WLC:Josh.9.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "peace covenant protects newcomers", "note": "Joshua makes peace with the Gibeonites, makes a covenant, and leaders swear to them; a community's word creates their protected status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33a7f67cd5a5824bbd66", "ref": "WLC:Josh.9.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an oath prevents violence", "note": "The commentary says the protected petitioner becomes inviolable; Israel does not strike the Gibeonites because its leaders swore an oath to them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d758d2455c68e7eacd5c", "ref": "WLC:Josh.9.19", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "oath restrains violence", "note": "The leaders say the sworn oath prevents the congregation from striking the Gibeonites, showing the protective force of a covenantal promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f8579f3560d3e189779", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "q-r-b: qurbah and qorban", "note": "The commentary explicitly says the hedy is brought as qurbah, nearness to God; Leviticus uses Hebrew qorban for an offering from herd or flock brought to YHWH, a cognate word family in a concrete sacrificial setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7cee96edc1bee9dd3290", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "herd or flock animal brought to YHWH", "note": "The commentary describes the hedy as an animal taken from a herd to the Haram; Leviticus likewise tells the worshipper to bring an offering from herd or flock to YHWH, while its cultic destination is the tent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f35ea5358e090b11dbd4", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "herd animal brought to the entrance", "note": "The commentary's hedy is taken from a herd to a sacred destination; Leviticus prescribes a male animal without blemish brought to the tent entrance as a voluntary burnt offering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-682a07055001b799efc6", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "herd offering at Tent entrance", "note": "A voluntary herd burnt offering is brought to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting for acceptance before YHWH, giving a more formal sacrificial arrival scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2b1f4d3d8e878948362", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "acceptance through the offered animal", "note": "The commentary calls the hedy an offering for nearness to God; Leviticus says the offerer lays a hand on the animal's head so it may be accepted to make atonement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02a0d757cbcec80a30ab", "ref": "WLC:Lev.17.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "livestock brought to Tent", "note": "An ox, lamb, or goat slaughtered for sacrifice must be brought to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting before YHWH; the animal's holy use depends on reaching the appointed place."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76b5e0d29af3ee6f4db9", "ref": "WLC:Lev.17.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "livestock brought to tent entrance", "note": "The commentary describes an animal carried to the Haram for offering; Leviticus requires Israelites to bring oxen, lambs, or goats to the tent entrance as offerings to YHWH."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17939d45aa6474fa29ab", "ref": "WLC:Lev.23.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed seasons and assemblies", "note": "The mo'adim are YHWH's appointed times and holy convocations, a calendrical gathering marked out in a way comparable to the commentary's season-sign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6264b87c4a0c85346f7a", "ref": "WLC:Lev.27.28", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ḥ-r-m: consecrated or forbidden", "note": "The commentary says a protected petitioner has hurma like the House's hedy; Hebrew ḥerem here is irrevocably devoted to YHWH and unavailable for ordinary sale, a cognate with a related consecration sense rather than the same sanctuary meaning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d20e8e5098bf2f42e28", "ref": "WLC:Mal.1.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "rejected cultic gift", "note": "God says he will not accept an offering from the speakers' hand; it supplies a different reason for an offered gift to fail to reach its intended acceptance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8fad15a15571c789456d", "ref": "WLC:Mal.1.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name, incense, and pure offering", "note": "The commentary brings the hedy offering together with God's name and the worlds; Malachi pictures nations from sunrise to sunset offering incense and a pure gift to YHWH's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b03f7c0ebe9a4b1901a", "ref": "WLC:Mal.1.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "pure offering to the Name", "note": "The prophetic vision of incense and a pure offering for YHWH's great name gives the name-and-offering conjunction developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3840de7abbe487dcd83", "ref": "WLC:Mal.1.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "male from flock and great Name", "note": "Malachi contrasts a vowed male animal withheld from sacrifice with YHWH's great name among nations, directly coupling flock, offering, and name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-302826a38fe59341e451", "ref": "WLC:Mal.2.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wife of covenant", "note": "Malachi calls the wife a partner and wife of covenant, giving a concise biblical equation of marriage with a binding protective relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-302d4eceadff437223fb", "ref": "WLC:Num.10.33", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "ark seeks a resting place", "note": "The ark goes before Israel to seek out a resting place, a covenantal guide moving the people toward a place where they may settle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af0cd1d3be5db1046642", "ref": "WLC:Num.2.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "banners, signs, and ancestral houses", "note": "Israel camps by standards and signs of ancestral houses around the Tent; marked communal gathering around a dwelling gives a concrete analogue to the season-as-sign image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c5f7204031806034d2d", "ref": "WLC:Num.35.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "asylum city for a fugitive", "note": "The commentary's petitioner seeks a group's protection and a safe destination; Numbers appoints cities to which an accidental killer may flee from the blood-avenger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0fb523cfb138c41c3a43", "ref": "WLC:Num.35.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cities for flight", "note": "Israel designates cities of refuge so that a manslayer may flee there; refuge is reached by travel to a named communal place."}
{"connection_id": "BC-975a6037b963a2a9dba3", "ref": "WLC:Num.35.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "refuge includes resident aliens", "note": "The refuge cities are for Israelites, resident aliens, and sojourners, making protected arrival available across communal status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1c26a3a8fc1d698f764", "ref": "WLC:Num.6.27", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's name placed on a people", "note": "The commentary says God's name is spoken over the hedy animal; this priestly formula places YHWH's name over Israel and promises blessing, applying the ritual name to the protected community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f668c6ee455f645fb1f", "ref": "WLC:Num.6.27", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Name placed for blessing", "note": "God says the priests place his name on Israel and he blesses them; the name functions as a bearer of benefit rather than merely an utterance over an animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-541b66c604b9f55f5f64", "ref": "WLC:Prov.18.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "gift opens access to the great", "note": "A person's gift makes room for him and brings him before great people, a concise wisdom-text account of a gift as mediated access to a recipient."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04b2c2b4d4b2b970827c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight road to a settled city", "note": "The commentary hears ihdinā as a request to reach the home at the path's end; these desert wanderers are led by YHWH on a straight route until they reach an inhabited city."}
{"connection_id": "BC-caeddbb06a2b42a0dd34", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straight way to inhabited city", "note": "God leads wilderness wanderers by a straight way until they reach a city to inhabit; the verse makes a straight road culminate in a place of belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe816c714fd31fb8ea19", "ref": "WLC:Ps.116.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sacrifice and invocation of the name", "note": "The commentary says God's name is spoken over the hedy and links it to worship; the psalmist offers a thanksgiving sacrifice while calling on YHWH's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de56341f27e7ffd9d853", "ref": "WLC:Ps.116.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sacrifice with invoked Name", "note": "The worshiper offers a thanksgiving sacrifice and calls on YHWH's name, an explicit analogue to sacrifice accompanied by divine naming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71cc0810ad092bab354c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.116.19", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vows fulfilled in the House", "note": "The commentary's path reaches a House and its season gathers people; the psalmist fulfills vows in the courts of YHWH's house amid Jerusalem's assembly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-161fcd214af456230cf6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.118.26", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "arrival in YHWH's name", "note": "The commentary joins a name spoken over an offering with arrival at the House; this pilgrimage greeting blesses the person who comes in YHWH's name to the sanctuary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-38010e6d1b37cc521ee5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.118.27", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "festal offering brought to the altar", "note": "The commentary's animal must reach its sacrificial destination; this verse is often read as binding the festal offering to the altar horns, though the Hebrew phrase's referent is disputed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa716f582f6c1bae0416", "ref": "WLC:Ps.122.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tribal pilgrimage and divine name", "note": "The commentary connects mawsim with a gathering and the House; here tribes go up to Jerusalem as Israel's testimony to give thanks to YHWH's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3df0ce71fa2182b78c2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.43.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide to holy dwelling", "note": "The commentary says ihdinā asks to be brought beyond the road toward its home; this speaker asks light and truth to guide him and bring him to God's holy mountain and dwelling."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8bca435d849c9b9ce43", "ref": "WLC:Ps.43.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "lead and bring to dwellings", "note": "The psalm asks that God's light and truth lead the speaker and bring him to God's holy hill and dwellings, closely joining guidance with arrival at a sacred home."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81b6d91ba696c060fe20", "ref": "WLC:Ps.45.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bride escorted to the king", "note": "The commentary reads h-d-y as a bride conducted to her husband's home; this royal wedding psalm says the princess is led to the king with companions following her."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b797a1d6158707bd91e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.45.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bride enters the king's palace", "note": "The commentary's bride is brought to her husband's house; the next wedding verse has the bridal party enter the king's palace with joy."}

