{"connection_id": "BC-d73b601a218614bbe24f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.45.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the king's name remembered", "note": "The commentary says the spoken name raises remembrance of the named one; this wedding psalm promises that the king's name will be remembered through generations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1d741eb39049db2f3cf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.47.8", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "melekh, king over all earth", "note": "The commentary's Arabic malik names God as sovereign; the Hebrew cognate melekh here declares God king over all the earth, while the commentary's marriage gloss remains a separate Arabic branch."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9e0134ca833f31c942b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.50.23", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanksgiving sacrifice and a rightly ordered way", "note": "The commentary joins worship, offering, and the request for guidance; this psalm says a thanksgiving sacrifice honors God and that the one who orders his way rightly will see God's salvation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f55904c03923f0b009c1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.53", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a flock led through the wilderness", "note": "The commentary joins an animal from a herd with divine guidance; this psalm says YHWH led Israel like sheep and guided them through the wilderness like a flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e4fa3f3cca0d75a66c4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.55", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the flock reaches holy land", "note": "The commentary pictures the guided route ending at a sacred home; the psalm says God brought the flock to the border of his sanctuary, to the mountain he claimed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c68baedd44b30fc39099", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew noam and Arabic n-ʿ-m", "note": "The commentary itself labels Arabic anʿamta, favor, and anʿām, herds, under n-ʿ-m; Hebrew noam here means YHWH's gracious pleasantness, a possible cognate for the favor sense but not for livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b70f0586e647049930bf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.96.8", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Name, offering, and courts", "note": "The worshiper is told to ascribe glory to YHWH's name, bring an offering, and come into his courts, a close formulaic counterpart to the commentary's named animal and house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d04e3ef9f9960808011", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.2.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge under God's wings", "note": "The commentary describes an outsider seeking a group's safe protection; Boaz blesses Ruth for coming to take refuge under YHWH's wings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29b1066a7584dbfce4b5", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.2.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "foreigner taking refuge", "note": "Boaz says Ruth has come to seek refuge under YHWH's wings after leaving her own people; it is a personal migration into protected covenantal belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b953f018e299e0b646fe", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.4.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "acquiring a wife", "note": "Boaz says that he has acquired Ruth as wife; it is a biblical legal idiom in which marriage is expressed through acquisition, relevant to the commentary's imlāk image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-513512dcbc42e40243ad", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.4.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the refugee becomes a bride", "note": "The commentary develops refuge and a bride brought to her husband's home; Ruth first seeks refuge as a foreigner, then marries Boaz and joins his household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dec0aa9038c138d6a308", "ref": "WLC:Song.1.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "king brings beloved into chambers", "note": "The speaker says the king has brought her into his chambers, a concise bridal arrival image in which the groom's dwelling is the destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-61c70fb56f56816a7990", "ref": "WLC:Song.8.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lover led into mother's house", "note": "The woman says she would lead her beloved into her mother's house; its reversed direction and household make it a useful contrary bride-and-home image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-551ed9f254fbba536d32", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.8", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "brought home under covenant", "note": "God brings scattered people to dwell in Jerusalem, and they become his people while he becomes their God in faithfulness; arrival and covenantal belonging are inseparable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d960cd47f3d36de4120d", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "prisoners return to stronghold", "note": "The prophet calls prisoners of hope to return to their stronghold, a compact return-to-safety image for threatened people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eedc82850f541d83be9c", "ref": "b. Rosh Hashanah 16a", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "the ram's horn recalls the binding", "note": "The commentary identifies the hedy as an animal offering; this rabbinic teaching interprets the shofar as recalling the ram at Isaac's binding and its merit before God."}

